WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-CA

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.040
What if I told you that the problem with&nbsp;
your writing is not that you're a bad writer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:09.520
but that you know too much? I ran a university&nbsp;
writing lab for seven years, and these days I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:16.320
work with nonfiction writers of all stripes. But&nbsp;
the problem is the same. The patterns that experts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:24.160
use to think about their subject fight with the&nbsp;
patterns that readers use to understand it. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:30.640
that fight has a name. It's called interference,&nbsp;
and it's the number one problem of expert writing.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.280
So in this video, I'm going to show&nbsp;
you why experts write the worst,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.560
how to spot interference&nbsp;
patterns in your own writing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:44.680
and how to fix them so that your ideas are&nbsp;
as clear to your readers as they are to you.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:48.760
So, what is interference, and why&nbsp;
does it happen? Well, quite simply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:54.800
interference is the mismatch between the&nbsp;
patterns that you use when you're generating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.880
the text and the patterns that readers use&nbsp;
whenever they're interpreting the text.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:05.360
See, that's the thing. If you're writing valuably,&nbsp;
then the kind of thinking that you're doing is way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:11.320
too complex to sort out in your head. It's a&nbsp;
little bit like Christmas lights. So you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:17.200
imagine having a set of Christmas lights that you&nbsp;
pull out once a year and hang up around the house.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.800
And you know how it is — they're always a&nbsp;
tangled mess. And before you can actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:28.360
put them on display, you need to go through&nbsp;
this toilsome process of untangling everything.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:35.440
And that's really what the first phase of writing&nbsp;
is. Whenever you're writing, it's not like an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:41.760
essay shape, or whatever genre you're using. It's&nbsp;
not in that shape in your head. It's a total mess.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:47.920
And you can't just go for a walk and sort&nbsp;
that mess out. It doesn't happen that way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:54.120
You need to, in the first place, use the writing&nbsp;
as an extension of your thinking. And if you do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:57.560
expert writing, then you know exactly what&nbsp;
I'm talking about — when you're sitting at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:02.400
the keyboard and you're kind of struggling&nbsp;
to figure out what it even is that you think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:07.400
before you can even think about putting&nbsp;
it into an essay shape for a reader.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:13.800
And so one way to diagram that would be to say&nbsp;
that you're generating. So here's the text that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:20.040
you and your reader are both going to use, and&nbsp;
you're generating it on this horizontal axis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:28.320
of time, where you're simultaneously thinking&nbsp;
about the world and writing about the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:35.440
And so the text is being generated according&nbsp;
to these patterns where you as an expert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:39.880
are thinking about the world. But the&nbsp;
problem with that is that readers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:46.800
using the text on a completely different axis.&nbsp;
They want to use the text to understand the world.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.920
So if you think about that expert writing process,&nbsp;
you're kind of moving through the text in time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:57.800
Think about it this way. You as the writer&nbsp;
start at the top of the text with a question&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:03.520
or a problem, something that you don't know,&nbsp;
because you're generating new ideas. And so you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:10.720
start at the top of the text not knowing, and you&nbsp;
kind of keep on chipping away at this draft until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:15.240
you get to the bottom of the text where you're&nbsp;
like, "Aha, I've got it now. It's complete."

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:19.600
But look at the pattern that you've&nbsp;
just generated. You've moved from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:24.160
a state of not knowing, a state of uncertainty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:30.920
to a state of certainty or knowing. And so&nbsp;
now if you just hand this off to your reader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:36.480
they need to follow your entire intellectual&nbsp;
journey from not knowing to knowing.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:42.240
Now, that's just one example of a writer&nbsp;
pattern that doesn't serve readers. You're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:47.600
not making a mistake. Everybody goes through&nbsp;
this. But the thing to just recognize is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:53.000
as you're generating this&nbsp;
text, you are simultaneously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:57.320
thinking about the world with your own&nbsp;
expert patterns. We're going to talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:02.600
those in a second. But those patterns make&nbsp;
it difficult for the reader to use your text.

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:07.680
And it's not to just say that the patterns&nbsp;
that writers use and readers use are different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:12.320
It's to say that the patterns that&nbsp;
writers use, especially experts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:17.080
which they use to generate the&nbsp;
text, interfere with the patterns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:23.600
the expectations, that readers use to process&nbsp;
the text. Readers do not want to move from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:28.760
uncertainty to certainty. For example,&nbsp;
they want the certainty right up front.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:32.360
And if you want an easy guide to the patterns&nbsp;
that readers use to process your text,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:36.480
then you can grab my visual guide to expert&nbsp;
writing down below the like button. It's four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:41.880
pages as a PDF, and it just gives you a&nbsp;
kind of road map to looking at your essay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:46.800
and thinking about it the way that readers&nbsp;
will. These aren't like my rules to writing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:52.440
These are just the kind of expectations&nbsp;
that readers bring to nonfiction writing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:57.040
It's all based on reader psychology. And so&nbsp;
if you can think about your text this way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:01.840
then it becomes really easy to move from that&nbsp;
first phase of writing, where you as the writer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:07.600
are generating the text while thinking about&nbsp;
it, transferring that over to the reader draft.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:12.120
But how can you spot interference in your own&nbsp;
writing? Well, you might think that one way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:17.880
that you could find it would be to give your text&nbsp;
to readers and see what they give you back. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:23.480
honestly, I think readers are the worst people&nbsp;
to talk to for feedback about your draft.

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:27.520
The reason that you don't ask readers for&nbsp;
feedback about your draft is for the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:31.960
reason that you don't ask a fish how to&nbsp;
catch a fish, or you don't ask a woman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:36.360
for dating advice if you're a man. Ladies,&nbsp;
do not pillory me in the comments for this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:42.400
but let me tell you that the worst dating advice&nbsp;
I've ever received has always come from women.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:46.840
Why is that? Well, I don't know for&nbsp;
sure, but what I do know for sure is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:51.120
what you say you want and what you&nbsp;
actually want are two different things.

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:56.440
I had a girl one time tell me that she wanted&nbsp;
a guy just like in the movie About Time. Have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.440 --> 00:06:00.880
you ever seen this movie? So I heard this,&nbsp;
and I watched this movie, and here's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:06.840
movie. It's about this dork who just can't&nbsp;
figure it out with women, but in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:12.600
he's got a time machine. And so every time he&nbsp;
fumbles the football, he can just hit undo and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:18.200
go back in time and try it again. And the entire&nbsp;
movie is him just fumbling, fumbling, fumbling.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:22.800
And girls watch that and they melt and they&nbsp;
say, "Oh, see, that's what I want." Well, guys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:27.640
go out there and try it. Watch that movie. Use it&nbsp;
like a playbook and see how that works for you.

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:32.800
And honestly, it works the same way with&nbsp;
readers. They'll tell you one thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.800 --> 00:06:37.920
but what they actually want is another thing.&nbsp;
So when your text doesn't work with readers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:42.960
here's what they'll tell you. They'll&nbsp;
say, "It's unclear." They'll say, "Oh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:48.520
it's disorganized. It's a little fuzzy.&nbsp;
It's dense." Remember back in university&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:53.800
when your professor would leave you a comment&nbsp;
in the margins that says "unclear, fix this"?

00:06:53.800 --> 00:07:00.280
The problem here is that readers don't have&nbsp;
the vocabulary to describe what's happening.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:06.200
So honestly, if you receive a comment like&nbsp;
"dense" or "unclear" or "disorganized,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:13.120
that advice is really worth nothing. It&nbsp;
doesn't tell you what to do. It just tells you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:19.160
the way that the reader is experiencing the&nbsp;
text. Readers don't have this language of saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:23.840
like, "Your writing patterns&nbsp;
interfere with my reading patterns."

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:28.600
And just understand that these words — dense,&nbsp;
unclear, disorganized, and those other kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:34.400
of general terms that you get in feedback about&nbsp;
your writing — those describe the symptom of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:41.320
writing, but they don't describe the cause. What&nbsp;
they're describing, in other words, is friction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:48.760
Readers want to move through your text. They want&nbsp;
to process it according to their reading patterns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:54.400
but those patterns are interfering with&nbsp;
the patterns that you've left on the page.

00:07:54.400 --> 00:08:00.520
One example that I've already provided is your&nbsp;
own intellectual journey from uncertainty to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:05.600
certainty. That's a pattern that's going&nbsp;
to interfere with the reader patterns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:11.520
Readers don't want to move from uncertainty to&nbsp;
certainty. They want to move from stability to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:16.920
instability. And that's a different video, but&nbsp;
it's just one of the reader kind of preferred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:22.680
motions or journeys through a text. They&nbsp;
want to begin in a familiar world and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:28.280
then discover that there's something wrong with&nbsp;
that world that they need to understand better.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:33.880
That's one of the reader kind of expectations&nbsp;
whenever they come to a piece of nonfiction.

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:39.920
So readers, a lot like women in the dating market,&nbsp;
don't have the best language for describing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:44.280
what it is they want. So if you get negative&nbsp;
feedback from them, take it all with a grain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:48.960
of salt and try to get to the bottom of&nbsp;
what it is that readers actually want.

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:52.280
And this concept of interference&nbsp;
is really important for writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:56.480
to understand, because when you get&nbsp;
that negative feedback from readers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:00.320
you don't want to wind up having&nbsp;
to work on the wrong thing.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:05.760
I'm actually working with a writer right now who's&nbsp;
writing a pillar essay for a coaching program&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:13.080
that he's about to launch. And so the essay is&nbsp;
kind of meant to introduce potential customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.080 --> 00:09:19.520
and the internet at large to a concept that he's&nbsp;
kind of owning in his personal brand. And again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:25.440
he's building a coaching educational offer&nbsp;
on the other end of it. But so what he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:30.360
done now — this is a very kind of niche topic,&nbsp;
and I've been working with him on this draft.

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:34.960
Now, he, like many writers whenever they come&nbsp;
to me, thought that the problem would just be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:41.400
a matter of line editing. So he would bring me&nbsp;
his draft and I would apply some patterns to it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:45.960
and turn it into a draft that works.&nbsp;
But it doesn't really work that way.

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:51.160
Like, I really think it's important to&nbsp;
think about the writer's draft and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:57.840
reader draft as two separate documents. You&nbsp;
have one draft where you as the expert are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:03.000
thinking about the world. You're thinking&nbsp;
about your ideas as you're generating the text,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:09.200
but then that text is going to mirror your&nbsp;
own patterns of thinking, which involve a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:15.720
of confusion and kind of recursive looping in on&nbsp;
itself, and fixing this, and getting pulled down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:21.640
that side trail, and trying this out. There's a&nbsp;
lot of experimentation and kind of iteration over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:26.840
you know, however long it takes you to generate&nbsp;
the text — many days at least — that goes into it.

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:33.000
And so you can't really fix that at the level&nbsp;
of line editing. I really think it's important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:38.480
like, once you as the writer are&nbsp;
satisfied, to walk away from that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:43.040
text. And that's really hard to do, because&nbsp;
you've invested so much of yourself into it.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:47.800
Working with this writer's draft, it's&nbsp;
a little bit like giving directions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:51.200
to a restaurant to a friend. So&nbsp;
imagine you're at a restaurant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:55.720
your friend's going to come meet you. You've&nbsp;
driven to the restaurant from your house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:59.600
and now your friend calls you and&nbsp;
they're asking for directions, right?

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:05.960
So what are you going to do? Are you going&nbsp;
to tell your friend the steps that you took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.960 --> 00:11:11.520
to arrive at the restaurant? That's not very&nbsp;
helpful to your friend, right? Your friend is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:17.960
expecting you to start your directions where&nbsp;
you understand they are, and to give them the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:24.640
appropriate level of context to let them know how&nbsp;
to get to the place where you've arrived, right?

00:11:24.640 --> 00:11:31.600
You use the text just like — you know, you&nbsp;
arrived at the restaurant following your own path,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:35.760
and that path served you well. Nothing&nbsp;
wrong with the path that you've taken.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:40.680
It's the steps that you needed to take in&nbsp;
order to arrive at this place of clarity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:46.880
But now you need to show the reader how to get&nbsp;
there. And you're an expert. The reader is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:52.680
starting their journey to the restaurant from&nbsp;
your home. You're starting from a completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:57.000
different walk of life. You have all these&nbsp;
other experiences, background knowledge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.000 --> 00:12:03.200
and just patterns of thinking that do&nbsp;
not suit the reader patterns of thinking.

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:09.440
And so in most cases, whenever an expert brings&nbsp;
me a text that they're preparing for a specific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:15.640
kind of reader, I think it's really hard for&nbsp;
them emotionally to accept that they need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:22.920
write a new draft. But honestly, that draft isn't&nbsp;
really so hard to write once you've done all your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:27.880
thinking. Like, once you've arrived at a place&nbsp;
where you think, "Okay, I have all the pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:34.640
that I need," then it's actually a lot easier to&nbsp;
map out a trail for the reader to get there. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.640 --> 00:12:40.040
the visual guide to expert writing is basically a&nbsp;
collection of all the patterns that I use whenever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:46.240
I'm thinking about how to prepare a reader to go&nbsp;
from where they are to where you want them to be.

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:52.720
Why is it that the more that you know about&nbsp;
a topic, the worse a writer you become? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:56.720
on the one hand, of course, you're the one&nbsp;
person in the world who should be writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.720 --> 00:13:03.160
about your topic, because you're an expert on it.&nbsp;
But on the other hand, because you're an expert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:07.480
you have compressed a lot of your knowledge into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:15.440
really tight kind of conceptual packets and&nbsp;
heuristics that become shorthands for you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:21.640
but become very dense and difficult to manage&nbsp;
for people who don't know what you know.

00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:27.920
And this is sometimes popularly known as the curse&nbsp;
of knowledge. But I don't want to use that phrase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:33.080
because honestly, for those of us who are in&nbsp;
the space of writing, that itself becomes one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:39.120
of these condensed packets of information, and&nbsp;
I think it's not exactly what I'm trying to say.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:44.120
The popular associations that we have with the&nbsp;
curse of knowledge — in case you're not familiar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.120 --> 00:13:48.760
this is a concept that comes from C.S.&nbsp;
Lewis, and it basically just means that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:54.360
you as a writer forget what it's like&nbsp;
to not know what you already know.

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:59.760
And so the advice that follows from this insight&nbsp;
about the curse of knowledge is to write simply,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:04.080
or write at a fifth-grade level. And that's&nbsp;
not the takeaway that I want to make,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:08.320
which is why I don't really want to use&nbsp;
that concept, the curse of knowledge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:15.040
Instead, I want to look at this issue to really&nbsp;
think about what happens when you're an expert.

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:20.800
So whatever the field is that&nbsp;
you're writing in, whatever it is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:28.400
your domain of expertise — the fact that you have&nbsp;
expertise in that domain means that you have gone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:35.080
through a lot of learning and condensing of&nbsp;
information. Like, when you learn something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.080 --> 00:14:40.440
you only need to learn it one time, and&nbsp;
then that becomes a kind of landmark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:44.960
or a post in the road for you, from where&nbsp;
you can begin the next time you're learning.

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:51.680
In other words, you start thinking about your&nbsp;
concept using these compressed kind of chunks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:57.320
of information that become like the&nbsp;
private machinery of your discipline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.320 --> 00:15:01.640
You know these concepts really well. You&nbsp;
take them for granted. And there are other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.640 --> 00:15:06.560
people who are in your space, colleagues&nbsp;
— especially if you're an academic. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.560 --> 00:15:10.800
it's true of all disciplines, but it's just&nbsp;
easy to think about this among academics.

00:15:10.800 --> 00:15:15.400
You know, I worked with a lot of academics for&nbsp;
seven years at the writing center, and what I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.400 --> 00:15:21.720
found is that these people are generally writing&nbsp;
for each other. And so there are all these kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:29.280
concepts and disciplinary norms that we take for&nbsp;
granted whenever we're writing to those people.

00:15:29.280 --> 00:15:34.840
But now, whenever you're tasked with the&nbsp;
problem of making your research valuable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:39.960
people who are not inside your discourse community&nbsp;
— which might be the lay public if you're trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:45.560
to write a popular book, but it could also be&nbsp;
committees who are issuing grants or funding, or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.560 --> 00:15:51.280
you know, private investors, that kind of thing&nbsp;
— you need to make the value of your research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:57.920
salient to them. And you forget what it's&nbsp;
like to not know everything that you know.

00:15:57.920 --> 00:16:02.280
So in one sense it means that yes,&nbsp;
there's a lot that you take for granted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:09.120
and so therefore you should present these items&nbsp;
simply. But it's more to say that as an expert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:16.000
you don't even realize the amount of compression&nbsp;
and the kind of weird thinking patterns that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:22.240
use within your discipline. And they're ones&nbsp;
that you take for granted among your peers. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.840 --> 00:16:26.640
whenever you're writing for people who are&nbsp;
outside of that disciplinary community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:32.000
it's important to think about what patterns&nbsp;
are they going to be using to interpret this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:36.600
because those are the patterns that you need to&nbsp;
use whenever you're trying to reach those readers.

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:41.480
And so the fix here is the advice that&nbsp;
unfortunately you really don't want to hear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.480 --> 00:16:48.120
but it's the truth — which is that you can't&nbsp;
think of writing as being a single act.

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:53.200
If you're an expert, if you are writing valuably,&nbsp;
if you are generating new knowledge, you're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:57.760
just regurgitating other people's ideas. You're&nbsp;
not doing summaries of other people's ideas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.760 --> 00:17:04.440
but you're actually trying to communicate&nbsp;
something valuable, which means you're solving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.440 --> 00:17:09.800
current problems, right? If you are solving open&nbsp;
problems, if you're answering open questions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.800 --> 00:17:16.840
it means you are using your writing as an&nbsp;
extension of your thinking. And so by definition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.840 --> 00:17:24.040
as an expert, you are going to be generating your&nbsp;
text along this axis where you are simultaneously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:30.000
thinking about the world and solving these&nbsp;
problems while you are generating that text.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:37.120
And so therefore you must get used to the idea&nbsp;
that there's no fixing that draft. There's no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.120 --> 00:17:43.640
amount of line edits — put a comma here, put these&nbsp;
two things into the same paragraph. It doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.640 --> 00:17:51.440
work. We can't fix these issues at the surface&nbsp;
level, at the formal level. It's a fundamental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.440 --> 00:17:58.920
issue of the way that this text was generated.&nbsp;
It was generated to serve your own thinking.

00:17:58.920 --> 00:18:04.520
But if you're trying to reach readers, if&nbsp;
you're trying to mobilize your expertise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.520 --> 00:18:08.640
into something that readers will find&nbsp;
valuable, then we need to generate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:14.320
a second text. I should almost draw a second&nbsp;
one over here. Here's the one for the reader.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:21.440
For the reader — and they're going&nbsp;
to use this to understand the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:27.480
right? This one is full of interference. They&nbsp;
can't use it. It was generated for you. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.480 --> 00:18:33.640
after we're done this one, we need to create&nbsp;
a second one for the reader that they can use.

00:18:33.640 --> 00:18:37.800
But here's one kind of concrete tip that you&nbsp;
can use whenever you are trying to fix these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:43.760
problems of interference. So, of course, grab&nbsp;
that PDF. But then one thing you'll notice in it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:51.273
is that I'm always referring to this fractal&nbsp;
pattern called the index and the discussion.

00:18:51.273 --> 00:18:57.979
And so that's kind of a general shape of&nbsp;
idea development. The index is where you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.979 --> 00:19:04.112
put a kind of high-level concept, and&nbsp;
the discussion is where you pay that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.112 --> 00:19:10.000
off. And I call it a fractal pattern&nbsp;
because it's a pattern that recurs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:16.240
at every level of resolution throughout the&nbsp;
text. So we see that index and discussion pattern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.240 --> 00:19:21.120
at the level of the entire text, where you have&nbsp;
your introduction and then the body. But then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.120 --> 00:19:25.880
when you zoom into the parts of the body, you&nbsp;
see that there's the index and discussion here.

00:19:26.400 --> 00:19:34.760
And the tip is to do what's called the skim test.&nbsp;
And so once you've kind of laid out your ideas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.760 --> 00:19:42.280
into this index-discussion pattern, what you&nbsp;
can do is highlight just the index positions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.280 --> 00:19:49.040
and see whether those create a kind&nbsp;
of storyboard for the major steps or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:56.200
rhetorical moves that you want the reader to move&nbsp;
through. Because the index is supposed to preview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.200 --> 00:20:03.000
the major piece of information in the entire unit&nbsp;
of discourse. And so the way that you can test&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:10.640
that, as a kind of diagnostic, is to see whether&nbsp;
just the index positions tell a coherent story.

00:20:10.640 --> 00:20:15.080
And so that skim test amounts to what's&nbsp;
known as a reverse outline. Right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.000 --> 00:20:20.520
Like, of course, a common piece&nbsp;
of advice is to create an outline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:26.920
for your text. But the problem with that is&nbsp;
that if you create the outline first, then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.880 --> 00:20:34.880
it's hard to fit especially expert patterns. Like,&nbsp;
as an expert, you're not just writing listicles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:40.400
right? You're not writing top five things. And&nbsp;
so it's hard to generate an outline first and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:46.080
then fill it in, because again, you're using&nbsp;
writing as an extension of your thinking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.080 --> 00:20:51.160
So one idea comes, and then you kind of expand&nbsp;
it and start exploring it, and then that might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:51.160 --> 00:20:55.640
take you down a little, you know, a little&nbsp;
tributary, and you go down that path, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:55.640 --> 00:20:59.880
right — so it doesn't really work so well if&nbsp;
you're trying to generate an outline first.

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:05.320
So what I recommend instead is: use your&nbsp;
writing in whatever patterns it occurs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.320 --> 00:21:10.600
you spontaneously. Like, let your mind wander&nbsp;
while you're writing. Absolutely do not think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.600 --> 00:21:17.840
about reading patterns while you're generating&nbsp;
your text. But then as you start to adapt the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.840 --> 00:21:22.720
weird thing that you've developed that served&nbsp;
your own thinking — when you start adapting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.720 --> 00:21:28.800
that for readers, you do need to make sure that&nbsp;
there's a kind of coherent structure to it all.

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:33.160
And so the way that you can do that&nbsp;
is with this reverse outline, right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:38.800
You can look at your text and then draw&nbsp;
lines demarcating the different sections.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.800 --> 00:21:44.760
And then within those sections, draw a line&nbsp;
that demarcates what's known as the index.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.760 --> 00:21:49.320
And then look only at those indexes and&nbsp;
see whether they tell a coherent story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:54.640
And if, as you're generating this reverse&nbsp;
outline, you cannot really label a part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.640 --> 00:21:59.880
well, it's probably still a tangled knot of&nbsp;
Christmas lights, or it's just fluff, right?

00:21:59.880 --> 00:22:06.200
Ideally, these indexes throughout the&nbsp;
piece are going to mark the major turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.200 --> 00:22:10.040
in your reader's journey. If you go&nbsp;
back to the metaphor about the reader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:14.720
finding their way to the restaurant,&nbsp;
right? These index positions should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.720 --> 00:22:19.560
like the major turns that they're going&nbsp;
to take on the way to the destination.

00:22:19.560 --> 00:22:23.360
But you can't really map out the reader&nbsp;
route if you don't know what patterns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:23.360 --> 00:22:26.960
they'll use to get there. So be&nbsp;
sure to watch this video next,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:26.960 --> 00:22:31.800
because it's going to show you the five reading&nbsp;
patterns that, if you follow them, is going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.800 --> 00:22:36.320
make your writing so much better from the reader&nbsp;
perspective. So be sure to go check it out.

